  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 212 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Today will be a chamber of commerce type of day with \nhigh temperatures warming into the lower to mid 60s after a cool \nstart to the morning. Light winds will switch to the south at 5-12 \nmph this afternoon. Plentiful sunshine will be seen through much of \nthe day with high clouds filtering in ahead of the approaching cold \nfront this afternoon. A cold front will move across the region \nMonday afternoon with the potential for isolated to scattered rain \nshowers ahead of the boundary. Models continue to show a plethora of \ndry air in the low levels and the general idea is that the best \nchances for rain will be across the high terrain as it's unlikely we \nsee the deeper valleys have time to saturate before dry air advects \ninto the region in the wake of the cold front. Some of the high-res \nguidance is showing up to 0.05\" of rain across the summits but even \nthat seems a bit generous given the antecedent dry air mass.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Noticeably colder temperatures will be observed on \nTuesday and Wednesday as high pressure and clear skies will \ncoincide with decent low level cold advection. Tuesday morning will \nsee lows in the 20s while Wednesday morning will see lows in the \nlower teens to lower 20s. Highs on Tuesday will only climb into the \n40s. It'll be our first real taste of arctic air this fall as the \ntransition to winter isn't too far away. As a matter of fact, we may \nbe looking at some record low temperatures Wednesday morning. The \ngood news is that high pressure overhead will allow the air mass to \nslowly moderate Wednesday into next weekend with highs climbing back \ninto the 60s for Thursday and Friday. There is a slight chance for a \nfew showers on Friday with another weak cold front but again it \nlooks like dry conditions will likely persist. For those with little \nones who plan to go trick-or-treating, the forecast for Halloween \nlooks dry and seasonably mild.",
